    <h1 style="color:#0f1f3d;">Diesel Generator Starting Batteries</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      A standby generator's battery is the component most likely to fail — not because it works
      hard, but because it sits idle for months and is expected to crank on demand. The chemistry
      is the same as a truck battery; the duty is not.
    </p>

    <div style="margin:20px 0;padding:16px 20px;background:#f4f7fb;border-left:4px solid #f5a623;border-radius:6px;">
      <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin:0 0 10px;font-size:1.15rem;">Direct Answer</h2>
      <p style="margin:0;">A standby diesel generator needs a starting battery matched to the engine&rsquo;s cranking requirement &mdash; often heavy-duty flooded or AGM with 850&ndash;1100+ CCA, and 24V on larger sets. The failure risk is not use but idle: a battery that sits for months self-discharges 3&ndash;5% a month and progressively sulfates, so a float charger and an annual load test matter more than a high CCA label.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Key Takeaways</h2>
    <ol class="takeaways-list">
      <li><strong>Idle is the enemy, not use</strong> — a battery with no load still self-discharges ~3–5% a month; a 135 Ah N150 sheds 9–15% of capacity in three idle months.</li>
      <li><strong>A float charger keeps it alive, not the CCA label</strong> — the alternator recharges a vehicle daily; a standby generator has no such duty cycle, so a charger or maintainer holds the charge.</li>
      <li><strong>Sulfation is the silent killer</strong> — a battery left below full charge sulfates progressively, losing capacity invisibly until the cold night it must start.</li>
      <li><strong>Test annually, replace proactively</strong> — 3–5 year service life is typical, but critical-start sites warrant annual load testing and planned replacement.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Why Generator Duty Is Different</h2>
    <p>
      A truck battery earns its keep every day: the engine starts, the alternator recharges it, and
      the cycle repeats. A standby generator sits in a bay for weeks or months, slowly self-discharging
      at 3–5% a month, then — the moment the grid fails or the control system calls for a start — it
      must deliver the full cold-crank burst instantly.
    </p>
    <p>
      The failure mode is invisible. As a battery sits below full charge, lead sulfate crystals form
      on the plates and harden, permanently reducing capacity. The operator sees no warning until the
      one critical morning the generator won't crank. The fix is disciplined: a float charger or
      maintainer that holds the battery at full charge, plus an annual load test that catches a
      weakening battery before it matters.
    </p>

    <h2 style="color:#0f1f3d;margin-top:32px;">Duty Cycle &amp; Environment</h2>
